insert into public.articles (slug, title, excerpt, body_md, tags, min_level, published_at)
values
  ('eating-in-front-of-other-people', 'Eating in Front of Other People', 'Work lunches, restaurants, family dinners: sometimes being watched while you eat makes your throat close. Why it happens, what quietly makes it worse, and how to widen what you can manage.', 'The sandwich is in front of you and you cannot make yourself start. There are four people at the table, one of them is your manager, and your throat has gone tight.

So you say you are not hungry, or you had something earlier, and you eat in the car afterward. It works, and it costs you a little more every time.

Why eating is an exposed thing to do

Eating in company involves most of the things that make people self-conscious at once.

Your hands are busy and visible. Your face is doing something you cannot see. There is noise, and mess, and a real chance of coughing, spilling, or having something stuck. You have to talk between mouthfuls, and swallow while being looked at.

On top of that, people have opinions about food, and plenty of us grew up being watched and commented on at the table: finish that, you are being fussy, is that all you are having. If eating was ever scrutinized, being watched while you eat is not a neutral thing.

What the body does

The alarm response is unhelpfully specific here. Your mouth goes dry, your throat tightens, your stomach closes down, and your hands shake slightly.

Which means swallowing genuinely is harder, and the shaking genuinely is there, so this is not simply a matter of imagining a problem. The sensations are real, and then the fear of them showing adds more adrenaline.

That loop is the thing to interrupt, and it responds much better to doing the situation in small doses than to trying to feel calm first.

What quietly makes it worse

Several of the natural strategies feed it.

Avoiding the occasions, which removes the chance to learn that nothing happens. Eating beforehand so you only have to pick, which keeps the fear intact. Sitting where nobody can see you. Drinking a lot to get the food down. Choosing only the safe foods, usually soft ones.

Watching yourself from the outside is the big one: monitoring your face, your chewing, whether anyone is looking. Attention turned inward makes every sensation louder and, as a side effect, makes you much worse at the conversation, which then looks like evidence.

Start with the easy versions

Build the ladder deliberately, from something you can nearly do to the thing you cannot.

That might be: eating with one person you trust at home, then a coffee and something small with a friend, then a quick lunch with a colleague, then a cafe at a quiet hour, then the work lunch, then the long family meal.

Stay on each rung until it is boring rather than until it is comfortable, which is a lower bar and a more honest one. Repetition is what does the work; a single brave attempt followed by three weeks of avoidance teaches nothing.

And when you do it, give the dropped strategies up one at a time. Eat the proper portion. Sit where you can be seen. Do not eat beforehand.

Practical moves at the table

Put your attention outward on purpose. Listen to what the person opposite is actually saying, look at the room, ask a question. This is not a distraction technique so much as putting your attention where it belongs.

Let yourself be slow. Nobody is timing you, and the people who finish first are not being judged well for it.

Breathe out longer than you breathe in before you start, and have a drink of water in reach, because a dry mouth is the one thing that genuinely makes swallowing harder.

Choose what you want rather than the safest thing on the menu, even once. The point is not the meal; it is teaching your alarm that the hard version is survivable.

What to say if someone asks

Usually nobody asks. When they do, a short line ends it.

I am a slow eater. I had a big breakfast. I am being careful with this shoulder. Or the plain truth if you want it: I get a bit anxious eating out, it passes.

You do not owe an explanation, and a brief one is far better than a detailed account, which tends to make the next meal feel more loaded.

If a family member comments on what or how much you eat, it is worth saying once, calmly and away from the table, that you would rather they did not.

When to get help with it

Please talk to a doctor or a therapist if this is shrinking your life: turning down work, avoiding friends, eating only alone, or losing weight because the situations are easier to skip than to face.

Mention it too if swallowing itself feels unsafe or you have ever choked badly, because a fear of choking has its own very treatable path and a doctor can rule out anything physical first.

This responds well to treatment, and the approach is mostly what is described here, done in order with someone keeping you honest about the avoidance. A counselor who works with anxiety will have met this many times and will not think it strange.', array['Food', 'Anxiety']::text[], 0, '2026-10-11T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('waking-at-three-in-the-morning', 'Waking at Three in the Morning', 'You are awake, your heart is going, and every problem looks unsolvable. Why the small hours are the worst possible time to think, and what to do instead of lying there.', 'You are awake, and you do not know why. Your heart is faster than it should be, and within about ninety seconds you are thinking about the money, or the conversation, or the lump, or all three.

By four you have reorganized your whole life and decided none of it will work. By eight, on the bus, the same thoughts are oddly manageable.

Why three in the morning is different

Several things line up against you at that hour, and none of them are about how bad your problems actually are.

Your body runs a cycle through the night, and in the early hours the stress hormone that wakes you up starts climbing. If you surface then, you surface into a slightly alarmed body, and the mind obligingly supplies a reason.

Sleep is also lighter in the second half of the night, so a full bladder, a noise, a warm room or a dream will lift you out of it more easily than at midnight.

And you are thinking with a tired brain in the dark, with no distraction, no daylight, nobody to check anything with, and nothing you can do about any of it until morning. That combination makes everything look both more urgent and less solvable than it is.

Do not try to solve anything

This is the single most useful rule, and it is hard to follow.

Whatever your mind presents at three, you cannot act on it, so working on it is not problem-solving. It is rehearsal, and rehearsal in the dark reliably produces worse conclusions than the same thought in the morning.

So the aim is not to settle the question. It is to decline the invitation, which is a different and more achievable thing.

A plain sentence helps, said in your head or quietly out loud: not now, this is a daytime problem. You will have to say it more than once.

Get your body out of the alarm

Because the wakefulness is partly physical, the body is where you have the most leverage.

Breathe out longer than you breathe in, for a couple of minutes, without making a project of it. Lengthening the out-breath is what calms the system down; trying to breathe deeply often does the opposite.

Unclench the obvious places: jaw, shoulders, hands, stomach. Let your weight be heavy on the mattress. If your feet are cold, cover them.

And let your eyes be soft rather than squeezed shut. You are not trying to force sleep, which never works, you are trying to be restful.

Get out of bed sooner than feels reasonable

If you are still awake and wound up after what feels like twenty minutes or so, get up.

Lying there awake and frustrated teaches your brain that bed is where you fret, and that association is what turns one bad night into a pattern. Getting up protects it.

Keep it dull and dim. No bright lights, no phone, nothing engaging. A chair, a blanket, a boring book, a radio at low volume. Then go back when you feel sleepy rather than when you feel you should.

Do not count the hours while you are up. The arithmetic of how much sleep you might still get is pure adrenaline.

Write it down and leave it there

Keep a pen and paper on the floor by the bed, not your phone.

When a thought keeps returning, write it in a few words and stop. Not the plan, just the item: call about the bill. Ask about the appointment. The point is to give your mind permission to stop holding it until morning, and writing does that in a way that promising yourself does not.

In the morning, read the list. Most of it will be smaller than it was at three, one or two things will be real and worth doing, and that is the useful part.

If it keeps happening

Look at the daytime, because repeated small-hours waking usually has ordinary feeders.

Alcohol in the evening is the most common one: it gets you to sleep and then wakes you in the second half of the night, exactly when this happens. Caffeine later than mid-afternoon, a hot room, and screens in bed all add to it.

So does having no time in the day when you think about the hard thing on purpose. People who never sit down with the worry get visited by it at three. Giving it fifteen minutes in the early evening, with a pen, genuinely reduces the night visits.

And keep your get-up time steady even after a bad night. Sleeping in to catch up is what pushes the next night later and keeps the cycle going.

When to get help

Please take it to a doctor if this is most nights, if you are exhausted in the day, if waking is coming with real breathlessness, chest pain or heavy snoring, or if the early waking has arrived alongside a mood that has dropped and stayed down.

There is a short, specific treatment for insomnia that works better than sleeping tablets, and a doctor or a therapist can point you at it. Say how many nights a week it is and what time you are waking, because that detail is what tells them which thing you are dealing with.

And if the three-in-the-morning version of your thoughts has turned to not wanting to be here, please tell a doctor or someone you trust in the daytime rather than carrying it alone. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Grounding', 'Sleep']::text[], 0, '2026-10-11T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('deciding-you-already-know-how-it-will-go', 'Deciding You Already Know How It Will Go', 'The interview will go badly, they will say no, it will not work out. How a confident prediction about the future quietly stops you trying, and how to test one instead of believing it.', 'You do not apply, because they will pick someone else. You do not ask, because they will say no. You do not mention the idea in the meeting, because it will get shot down.

None of that feels like avoidance. It feels like being realistic, which is why it is so effective at keeping your life smaller than it needs to be.

The prediction arrives as a fact

The reason this one is hard to catch is that it does not announce itself as a guess.

It arrives in the same flat, certain tone as a memory: that will not work. Nobody will come. She will be annoyed. And because it feels like knowledge, you do not examine it, you plan around it.

A tired or low mood makes the predictions bleaker, and a frightened one makes them more specific. Neither adds any actual information about Thursday.

Where it costs you

The damage is rarely the thought itself; it is the thing you quietly did not do.

The job you did not apply for, the invitation you did not send, the price you did not ask for, the apology you did not offer, the appointment you did not make. Each one is a small decision that felt sensible and removed the only route to a different answer.

And because you never ran the test, the prediction is never contradicted, so it stands. That is the real trap: avoidance protects the belief that made you avoid.

Write the prediction down properly

Vague gloom cannot be checked, so make it specific enough to be wrong.

Not it will go badly, but: they will interrupt me in the first two minutes, I will have no answer, and they will say no by Friday. Not nobody cares, but: I will invite six people and fewer than two will reply.

Give it a number if you can, and a date. The act of writing it this plainly often takes some of the certainty out on its own, because stated flatly, most predictions sound more like a fear than a forecast.

Then keep the paper, which is the part people skip.

Run the smallest possible test

You do not need to bet the whole thing. You need one piece of evidence.

Send the message to one person instead of six. Ask the question to the colleague you find easiest. Apply for the version of the job you are not attached to. Raise the idea at a small meeting rather than the big one.

Then compare what happened to what you wrote, in plain terms, without editing it afterward into a near miss.

What usually comes back is a messy, partial result: they did not say no, but they did not say yes; two people replied and one was keen; the question was fine and the answer was unhelpful. Reality is almost never as clean as the prediction, and that in itself is the lesson.

Count the times you were wrong

People are good at remembering the predictions that came true and losing the rest, which keeps the habit well fed.

So keep the running list for a few weeks. Prediction, what actually happened, one line each. Most people find that a surprising proportion of theirs were either wrong or roughly half-right, and that the ones that came true were rarely as bad as the forecast.

Notice too what you do with a good outcome. If the explanation is always that you got lucky, or they were being kind, or it does not count, then the evidence is being thrown away before it can change anything.

Separate the prediction from the plan

Being realistic has a real job to do, and it looks different from this.

A plan says: this might not work, so here is what I will do if it does not. It stays in contact with the actual odds and it leaves the attempt intact.

A prediction says: it will not work, so there is no point starting. It feels like the same caution and it removes the attempt.

The test is whether the thought leads to a precaution or to a cancellation. Precautions are useful. Cancellations are the thing to be suspicious of.

When it is more than a habit of thought

If this has settled into most areas of your life, if the certainty is so heavy that trying anything feels pointless, or if the predictions are mostly about yourself rather than the situation, that is worth working through with someone.

Please talk to a doctor or a therapist if a flat, hopeless certainty about how things will go has been with you for weeks, or if it has stopped you doing things you care about. This is one of the most well-mapped areas there is: a counselor will work through exactly the steps above with you, and having someone keep you honest about the tests makes a large difference to how fast it shifts.', array['Thinking Traps', 'Motivation']::text[], 0, '2026-10-11T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('asking-someone-to-stop-giving-you-advice', 'Asking Someone to Stop Giving You Advice', 'Unasked-for suggestions from a parent, a friend or a colleague, over and over. Why the advice keeps coming, how to ask for it to stop without a row, and what to do when it does not.', 'You mention that you are tired and you get a list: have you tried going to bed earlier, have you had your iron checked, you know what you should do.

It is meant kindly, usually. And after the fortieth time you stop telling that person anything, which is a loss neither of you chose.

Why it keeps coming

Most unasked-for advice is anxiety with its sleeves rolled up.

When someone cares about you and cannot fix what is happening, suggesting things is the available action. It settles them, which is why it keeps arriving regardless of whether it helps you.

Some of it is habit from an older arrangement: a parent who solved your problems for twenty years does not notice when the role expired. Some of it is how a person shows love, badly. And a little of it is enjoying being the one who knows.

None of those require you to keep receiving it.

Decide what you actually want instead

This is worth a minute''s thought, because "stop giving me advice" leaves a gap and people fill a gap with more of what they were doing.

Usually what you want is one of a few things. To be listened to without a solution. To be asked before suggestions arrive. To talk about something else entirely. Or to be helped with a specific thing and nothing else.

Name it to yourself first, then you can ask for something rather than only object to something, which is much easier for the other person to do.

The short version to say

Keep it brief, warm, and about you rather than about their character.

I know you are trying to help. What I need at the moment is just to talk it through, not to be given things to try. Can we do that?

Or: I will ask if I want suggestions. Until then, assume I have thought about it.

Or, for the recurring topic: I have made my decision on this one. I would rather not keep reopening it.

Then stop talking. The urge to soften it with three more sentences is strong and it usually undoes the request.

Say it when nothing is happening

Timing matters more than wording.

Said in the middle of an argument, or straight after a piece of advice, it lands as a rebuke. Said on a walk, in the car, over a cup of tea, when the subject is nowhere near, it lands as information.

And say it once, properly, rather than in small irritated pieces over months. Hints do not work on this; they read as moods.

What to do when it keeps coming

Expect to repeat yourself a few times, and have a short line ready so you are not inventing one each time.

Thank you. I am not looking for ideas on it. Then change the subject in the same breath, because a pause is where the next suggestion goes.

You can also stop supplying the material, which is not sulking, it is sensible. If every mention of your job produces a lecture, talk about other things with that person and talk about your job with someone who listens well. Not everyone gets to be your sounding board on everything.

And notice the difference between someone who forgot and someone who heard you and carried on. The first needs reminding. The second needs less access to the subject.

If it is a parent

This is the hardest version, and a slightly different one.

Advice from a parent often carries an old message underneath it: that you are not quite managing. That is what stings, more than the content, so it is worth saying the real thing rather than arguing about the suggestion.

Something like: when you tell me how to handle my own house, it makes me feel about fifteen. I know you do not mean it that way.

Expect it to take several goes, and expect some hurt. Most parents adjust when the request is specific, repeated, and not delivered as an accusation. Some do not, and then the work is on your side: shorter updates, fewer topics, and letting the suggestion go past you without a reply.

When it is wearing you down

If this is coming from someone you cannot avoid, if it is part of a wider pattern of being overridden or criticized, or if you dread seeing a particular person because of it, that is worth taking seriously rather than tolerating.

Please talk to a therapist or a counselor if saying no to this person feels impossible, if you leave every conversation doubting your own decisions, or if the advice is really control with a kind voice. Those patterns are workable, and a professional can help you find the lines and hold them.', array['Assertiveness', 'Boundaries']::text[], 0, '2026-10-11T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('crying-more-easily-than-you-used-to', 'Crying More Easily Than You Used To', 'An advert, a small kindness, a song in the car. When tears start arriving at nothing in particular, it is usually telling you something about your capacity rather than about you.', 'You are fine. Then someone holds a door, or there is a dog on the television, and your eyes go, and you have to look out of the window until it passes.

It is embarrassing and slightly bewildering, especially if you have never been someone who cries easily. It is also one of the most reliable signals the body gives, and worth reading rather than fighting.

What the easy tears usually mean

Crying at small things is rarely about the small thing. It is a sign that the system is running with no slack in it.

Holding feelings down takes effort, and that effort comes out of the same account as everything else. When the account is low, the lid does not fit properly, and whatever is underneath comes out at the first thing that touches it. Often that is kindness, which is why unexpected gentleness sets people off more than bad news does.

So the tears are information: there is more being carried than there is capacity to carry it. They are not a sign that you are falling apart, and they are not a character change.

What lowers the threshold

Several ordinary things do it, and they stack.

Not enough sleep, for more than a few days. Pain. Being ill, or just getting over something. Hunger, and skipping meals while busy. Alcohol, which lifts the lid a day later rather than at the time.

Longer stretches of strain do it too: caring for someone, a stretch of overwork, a house move, a grief you have not had time to sit in. Hormonal changes across the month, after a birth and around menopause have a clear and unembarrassing effect on this as well.

Look at the last two weeks before you look at yourself. Usually three or four of those are present.

Crying is not the problem to solve

The instinct is to stop it happening. That is the wrong target, for two reasons.

Suppressing it works briefly and raises the pressure, so it arrives later at a worse moment. And the crying itself is doing something useful: it tends to be followed by a drop in tension, which is what people mean when they say they felt better after a good cry.

So where you can, let it happen somewhere you are not performing: in the car, in the shower, with the one person who does not make it a crisis. Ten minutes of actually crying is often worth more than three days of holding it.

What to do when it arrives at work

You still need something for the moments when it is not convenient, and small physical moves work better than trying to think it away.

Breathe out slowly, twice, and let your shoulders drop. Press your feet into the floor. Hold something cold, or run your wrists under cold water. Look at something far away rather than at the person in front of you.

If you need a minute, take one. I need two minutes, I will be back is a complete sentence in almost any workplace.

And if it happens in front of someone, a short line prevents the whole thing becoming a conversation: sorry, I am running on about four hours sleep, I am alright. Then carry on.

Refill what is empty

This is the part that actually changes it, and it is less about managing feelings than about capacity.

Sleep first, because nothing else works while sleep is short. Then food at regular times, then some movement, then a genuine gap in the week with nothing scheduled in it.

Then look at what is being carried and whether any of it can be put down for a while, handed over, or postponed. People in this state usually say no to nothing and that is where the load comes from.

And give the real thing somewhere to go: a conversation with someone who listens, twenty minutes with a pen, a walk where you think about it on purpose. Feelings that have a time and a place turn up less often uninvited.

If you cannot cry at all

The opposite version is just as common and gets even less attention.

Feeling flat, blank, and unable to cry even when something sad happens is also a sign of an overloaded system rather than a cold one. It often comes after a long stretch of coping, and it tends to thaw slowly once there is some rest and some safety.

It is worth mentioning to someone rather than waiting for it to pass on its own, particularly if nothing has felt like much of anything for several weeks.

When to take it to someone

Please talk to a doctor if this has gone on for more than a couple of weeks, if it arrived with a low mood, broken sleep or a change in appetite, or if you are exhausted in a way that rest is not touching.

There are physical reasons for a sudden change in how easily you cry, including thyroid trouble, iron levels, pregnancy, and the start or change of a medication, and those are quick to check and worth ruling out.

A therapist is the right place for the other half of it: the load you are carrying, and what might come off it. Crying easily is one of the most common reasons people finally book an appointment, and it is a sensible one.

And if the tears are coming with a feeling that you do not want to be here, please tell a doctor or someone you trust today. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Emotion Regulation', 'Burnout']::text[], 0, '2026-10-11T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz)
